        "text": "We are using more resources annually than the Earth can renew in a year.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The current rate of resource consumption exceeds planetary regeneration capacity.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Humanity currently consumes resources equivalent to what would require 1.6 Earths to sustainably regenerate.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Planetary boundaries for ecological sustainability exist and are measurable.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Resource use should align with Earth’s regenerative capacity to maintain long-term viability.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The concept of 'Earth Overshoot Day' provides a valid metric for measuring ecological deficit.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Audiences understand and accept the metaphor of multiple Earths as a meaningful representation of overshoot.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Sustainable development requires reducing per capita resource consumption globally.",
        "confidence": 0.85
