        "text": "Democratic systems should ideally enable collective decision-making on critical societal issues.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Progress on climate change requires consensus or majority support to implement meaningful policies.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The legitimacy of democratic governance depends on its ability to act effectively on urgent threats.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views the current political system as being vulnerable to obstruction by small groups.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Effective leadership involves navigating institutional constraints without sacrificing core objectives.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 82241,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82241.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 62,
    "turn_text": "I believe the way we make progress is by delivering, by showing people very specifically what the benefits are, what the facts are, and by building a consensus. I mean, that is the process of democracy. It's been made harder in these last years because denialism that has been exacerbated purposefully by entities and by politicians. But I do believe the marketplace actually has the ability to be a very powerful force for good and for things to happen. And politicians can kind of get the way and provide some road bumps, but they're not going to stop what's happening now.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
